msgctxt "seo_title"
msgid "Tepito: A Neighborhood Governs Itself"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "search_description"
msgid ""
"A dispatch from Mexico City's legendary barrio reveals how true innovation—from sound-system message networks to black-market tech adaptation—happens far beyond Silicon Valley. Filmmaker and "
"technologist Violeta Ayala explores how self-governance, resistance, and raw intensity shape the future of technology and human connection."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "title"
msgid "Tepito: A Neighborhood Governs Itself"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "hero_image_alt_text"
msgid "Image of a warehouse having the name Tepito painted on its wall."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "lede_text"
msgid "The future will not be made by billionaires. They don't know how to invent genres or create new ideas. A chronicle from a neighborhood that governs itself — contradictions and all."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.58a1c31f-7d5a-4624-ba12-fd466e0b31d9"
msgid ""
"I'm writing this at Mexico City's Benito Juárez airport. I read a place by what surrounds it and by what it does to my body, before anyone explains it to me. I was in Mexico with a group of "
"scientists and developers. All of them geniuses. Everything was intense, and we spent the gathering in la Condesa, but on the last day I went to Tepito to visit an Australian friend. It only takes "
"forty minutes to go from the gentrified Condesa to Tepito or however long the metro takes."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.58a1c31f-7d5a-4624-ba12-fd466e0b31d9"
msgid ""
"I work in technology, film, and robotics. I spend my time in rooms where people debate who is going to build the future — with which models, with what capital, with whose permission — and then I "
"land in a barrio that already built it without asking anyone."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.58a1c31f-7d5a-4624-ba12-fd466e0b31d9"
msgid ""
"The name Tepito comes from Náhuatl. Cecilio Robelo traced its origin to <i>Teocaltepiton</i> — «small temple» — after a little temple that stood on the site, still called San Francisco "
"Teocaltepiton, or Tecuahltepiton, in the colonial period, until phonetic contraction left only Tepito. Fray Alonso de Molina and Rémi Simeón agree: <i>tepiton</i>, something small. The barrio was "
"named for a small temple. The temple never left."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.9e675fa0-0737-4096-b3c6-5f9447f9e7f5.quote"
msgid ""
"Where la Santa Muerte has an altar on Alfarería and people bring her cigarettes, apples, tequila — the temple only changed saints. Where two teenagers kiss in the street without discretion. Where "
"kids play fútbol against a wall with a goal painted on it. Here the music is invented. Where señoras in their seventies are dressed better than any fashion show. Three micheladas for a hundred pesos"
" (5 USD). Everything loud, everything specific, nothing waiting for permission."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.fe8e4ed5-bb0c-4d9c-8ff2-f1c96d9eda24.image.alt_text"
msgid "By Violeta Ayala"
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.f09554b4-988a-4f49-8be9-8a0a6f282c2a"
msgid ""
"Live with this much intensity and a hundred and fifty years is too much. In la Condesa there are people optimizing their sleep to reach a hundred and fifty. Further north, in Silicon Valley, there "
"is an obsession with preservation. On Alfarería nobody is asking for the extra century. Death is respected here."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.f09554b4-988a-4f49-8be9-8a0a6f282c2a"
msgid ""
"Where the live DJ plays cumbia and reads the saludos out loud between songs. Dedications written on slips of paper and handed over by hand. A greeting to a cousin in Chicago. To a brother inside the"
" Reclusorio Norte. To someone's mother in Puebla who will hear about it tomorrow from a neighbor. A message-passing network running on a rented sound system. No logs. No owner. It can't be acquired."
" It has moved more meaning across borders than most of the platforms we spend our lives building and auditing."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.f09554b4-988a-4f49-8be9-8a0a6f282c2a"
msgid ""
"La rebajada already proved it. Dueñez's sound system overheated after six hours and the record started turning slower. He tried to fix it, couldn't, and the crowd kept dancing, so he left it. In "
"Houston at the same moment, DJ Screw was doing it on purpose: two copies of the same record, pitch dragged down, the crossfader chopping beats and phrases, the whole thing recorded to a master "
"cassette and then slowed again on a four track. He sold the tapes out of his house on the Southside. Both start in the early nineties. Both travel on cassette, hand to hand, outside any label."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.f09554b4-988a-4f49-8be9-8a0a6f282c2a"
msgid ""
"A malfunction turned into a whole new genre. In Houston, DJ Screw had a similar creative process at the same time — two communities discovering that slowing recorded time down opens a sensory space "
"that didn't exist before. The invention came from the ear and from material scarcity.<br/><br/>Copying works the same way: Shenzhen's shanzhai workshops were called pirating until the reverse-"
"engineering base beneath them produced DJI and Huawei. La fayuca, contraband, is how technology reaches the places capital refuses to serve. The machine crosses the border in somebody's suitcase and"
" lands in a barrio, and the people who buy it there decide what it's for. El fayuquero brings the machine. El sonidero decides what it plays. The machine (A speaker and an amplifier) arrive in "
"Tepito in somebody's luggage. The factory that built them was thinking of a living room. The sonidero who buys them turns them into a public address system for the whole block, and then into a way "
"of talking to a cousin in Chicago. The machine is imported. The use is made here. The machine arrives with no manual and no warranty. People open it, repair it with what they have, and put it to "
"work on something its manufacturer never considered. El fayuquero and el sonidero do continuous work."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.4399ed77-38e9-406f-910c-2d207111aa95.image.alt_text"
msgid "Author Violeta Ayala in Tepito in front of a Truck "
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.898a911b-357b-45ff-9b2d-6bb85ebb9489"
msgid ""
"Cash works like this, too: No behavioral profile, no credit score, no dataset sold to a broker. Fayuca and tianguis: an economy that works and leaves no trace. Data minimization, implemented for a "
"century by people who never came to the working group."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.898a911b-357b-45ff-9b2d-6bb85ebb9489"
msgid ""
"Cash also makes el cobro de piso possible. It's the charge for the floor, the payment that buys you protection and the right to have a stall. You pay it every week for as long as you sell. Nobody is"
" outside it. (It's the operating condition of the whole market, happens in most of the markets in Latin America, Taiwan, makes autonomy possible but its also a kind of extortion) The same autonomy "
"carries the extortion. The economy that refuses surveillance also has no pensions, no recourse, no ambulance that comes fast. In Andean though we have a word, <b><i>ch'ixi</i></b>: something that is"
" two things at once and stays that way, without resolving, without turning gray. Tepito is <b><i>ch'ixi</i></b>. It's not a model to deploy. A version that keeps the michelada and drops the violence"
" would be one more extraction, easier to consume than the barrio itself."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.898a911b-357b-45ff-9b2d-6bb85ebb9489"
msgid ""
"The barrio has carried that contradiction for centuries. Mecamalinco, one of its old names, was among the last places where the Mexica and the Tlatelolca resisted Cortés. When they fell, the site "
"was named Tequipeuhcan: «place of slavery». Five hundred years of governments wanting to make the barrio disappear —reorder it, demolish it, ship its people to the east of the city— and it's still "
"there, taping up posters about dignity on the walls. It has survived every prediction of its own end."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.898a911b-357b-45ff-9b2d-6bb85ebb9489"
msgid ""
"<a id=\"a1\">Elon Musk</a> tells himself he is a Cassandra: he sees the future better than anyone and is doomed not to be believed. «What I say will come to pass», he insists. He predicts that "
"within five years artificial intelligence will surpass the sum of all human intelligence. The sum. As if intelligence were a quantity you could total and then exceed. Musk can't understand Tepito, "
"because the intelligence of the uncounted — those of us who were never a unit, those of us who have survived— is not a sum. It's carrying the contradiction without resolving it. It's staying alive "
"in the place they named slavery. It's inventing a genre with a broken motor. No superintelligence surpasses that, because it was never a number."
msgstr ""

msgctxt "body.898a911b-357b-45ff-9b2d-6bb85ebb9489"
msgid ""
"The future of technology won't be built by billionaires. They can't invent la cumbia sonidera. They don't know how to live with intensity. They're too busy not getting older. No community, no "
"responsibility, only a bubble they've convinced themselves is the world. They don't have a chance."
msgstr ""

